questionnaire app,

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.980
achieve 20% saving
while deploying

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:54.140
to hundreds of
medical institutions

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:55.940
and improving availability.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.560
Or Scoopwhoop, a media platform
for whom autoscaling on GKE

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.860
helps match capacity with
the real-time demand.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:07.340
The result is 99%
uptime and 50% reduction

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:11.270
in infrastructure costs, while
removing operational burdens.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:15.950
Now they can invest more
time on coding and less

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.200
on managing the resources.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.010
As well as Mr. Cooper, an
industry-leading mortgage

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:26.090
service provider that now can
pack their nodes to achieve 90%

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:27.560
CPU load.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.800
In fact, Forrester
conducted a study

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.400
in which they created a
composite organization based

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.370
on the GKE customers
and concluded

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:41.060
the following total economic
impact numbers of GKE

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.970
with expected investment
payback in under six

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.640
months for a typical customer.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.370
Check their study to learn more.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:51.410
Over to Roman.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:53.300
ROMAN ARCEA: Thank you, Jerzy.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.020
This is great.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.620
Our team was on a critical
mission, a mission

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.140
to empower you,
the user, to never

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.810
again waste time and money
on infrastructure you

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:04.730
don't actually use.

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:07.340
When you run workloads on
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.500
we know you've made
the choice in favor

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:13.610
of simplicity, efficiency,
security, and scalability.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:17.000
But we also know that falling
for the non-efficiency pitfalls

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.580
is easy when you are in charge
of running infrastructure

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.920
at scale, with its endless
number of components

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:23.300
and possibilities.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.180
In six years of
GKE's existence, we

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:30.450
learned that four major
challenges make your life hard.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.600
The first challenge is cultural.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.660
Many teams that embrace the
public cloud aren't used

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.240
to the pay-as-you-go
billing style and frequently

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.610
don't fully understand the
environment their apps are

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.250
running on, in this
case Kubernetes.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:45.950
The FinOps movement, which
is getting lots of attention

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:48.800
recently, is all about
evolving such a culture.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.900
One of the FinOps
best practices is

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.240
to provide teams with
real-time information

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.790
about their spending and
their business impact.

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:58.430
Small things like this
have considerable impact

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.550
on companies' culture, resulting
in a more balanced cost

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:03.860
optimization equation.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:05.630
The second challenge
has been packing.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:07.460
How well do you
pack applications

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:09.320
onto your Kubernetes nodes.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:13.310
The better you pack apps onto
nodes, the more you save.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:15.380
Then we have the
app rightsizing,

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:18.890
the ability to set appropriate
resource requests and autoscale

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:21.110
applications deployed
in the cluster.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:23.810
The more precisely you set
resources on your pods,

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:26.390
the more reliably your
applications will run,

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:29.720
and in the vast majority
of cases, the more space

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:31.760
you'll save in the cluster.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.400
And the last challenge is
node scaling down your cluster

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:35.870
during off-peak hours.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:39.110
Ideally, to save money
during low-demand periods,

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:41.030
for example at
night, your cluster

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:44.540
should be able to scale down
following the actual demand.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:47.630
However, in some
cases, scaling down

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:49.940
doesn't happen as
expected due to workloads

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:54.030
or cluster configurations
that block cluster autoscaler.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.580
By themselves, each
of those are tricky.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.670
But together, this
is a complex problem.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:02.790
In our experience, most of
our provisioned environments

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:06.530
have at least two of the
following challenges at hand.

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:07.550
But fear not.

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:09.330
We're here to help.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.840
Let's look at some of the
incredibly powerful GKE

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:13.850
capabilities you
might have missed

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:17.870
that will take you a step closer
to running GKE at scale cost

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:19.480
effectively.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:21.600
Jerzy, over to you.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.460
And tell us about your
favorite top three.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:25.110
JERZY FORYCIARZ:
Thank you, Roman.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:28.290
I certainly have
my own top three.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:31.920
Let me focus on some of the
existing top-of-the-line GKE

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:33.330
capabilities.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:36.860
And here there are.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:38.730
Autopilot.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:42.060
Traditional Kubernetes
expects you, the user,

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.780
to understand and manage the
control plane, worker node,

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:50.130
security configuration, upgrade,
scaling, and availability.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:53.760
GKE, on the other hand,
is a truly managed service

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.550
that helps you focus
on your business.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:05:00.360
The best and most intelligent
way of using Kubernetes today

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:04.200
is by activating GKE Autopilot,
a mode of operation that

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:07.140
automatically manages
the most complex aspects

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.600
of your infrastructure
configuration.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:15.030
If, however, the user needs
more control of their clusters,

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:19.460
she can choose a standard
GKE mode of operation.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:24.650
You can think of modes of GKE as
the level of control customers

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:26.900
have over a given GKE cluster.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.340
And the best part yet?

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:30.860
You can mix and match
modes of operation

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:33.480
between different clusters.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:38.300
So you choose GKE Standard
for advanced configuration,

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:41.450
flexibility over the
cluster infrastructure,

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:45.530
or GKE Autopilot to
have Google provision

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:48.860
and manage entire
underlying infrastructure.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:52.850
User is in full control
and can break glass,

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:57.090
converting Autopilot
clusters into Standard.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:01.140
These are just a few examples
of how Autopilot does things

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:03.090
differently.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:06.660
Autopilot clearly wins as a
hands-off Kubernetes experience

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:10.140
because it optimizes for
production like Kubernetes

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:15.150
expert, provides strong
security posture,

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:19.080
lets Google become your SRE,
reducing the day-2 operation,

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:22.620
improves resource
efficiency, and it's still

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.650
Kubernetes, still GKE.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:32.840
But how does this all play
into GKE cost optimization?

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:37.490
GKE Autopilot shares a
four-way scalability capability

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:40.010
with the GKE Standard.

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:44.120
Same, well-known tech as
horizontal pod autoscaling

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:47.120
or vertical pod
autoscaling apply and serve

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.220
as a frame of cost reference.

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:53.270
Users are still in full control
about how their workloads will

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:54.800
scale and behave.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.860
However, the
infrastructure is now

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:00.230
managed fully by the Autopilot.

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:04.160
And that has significant
optimization implications

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:08.320
for everything you
plan to do with GKE.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:10.190
You remember early
in the conversation,

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:12.550
we talked about the
four main challenges

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:14.730
of cost optimization.

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:18.390
With Autopilot, you pay for the
compute resources requested.

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:22.530
With our new pay-per-pod
model, paying for nodes

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:25.900
remains a thing of the past.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:29.910
That means that bin packing
is not a problem anymore.

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:31.850
How cool is that?

00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:35.180
Not only do you get a
managed hands-off experience

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.100
that lets you focus
on your business

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:40.460
while improving your security
and operational posture,

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:43.700
you also get rid of one of
the most challenging aspects

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:45.900
of cost optimization.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:49.290
And if you thought
this is not enough, how

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:52.320
about not having to pay for
the compute overhead costs

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.470
of running the OS and the
cost of running Kubernetes,

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:57.600
such as Kube system components?

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:03.790
Scalability is a big subject.

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:07.660
We graduated 15k to
GA in 2021, which,

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:10.480
for the vast majority of
users, removed scalability

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:13.440
as a constraint
in cluster design.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.210
Why does it matter?

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.550
Thanks to operating
in one large cluster,

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:21.750
you can simplify microservices
lifecycle management,

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:25.080
easier absorb larger
spikes in resource demand,

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:28.470
and shorten the time to process
data for batch workloads.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:31.560
As the result, you can
combine the services

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:35.940
of your organization into one
or just a few GKE clusters that

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:41.830
provide better utilization
and, in effect, will cost less.

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:43.990
Combine this with
our cost optimized

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:47.650
machine types such
as E2 or Tau for even

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:51.520
better price/performance ratio
compared to general purpose

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:52.240
VMs.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:55.600
Next is the node
auto-provisioning,

00:08:55.600 --> 00:09:00.130
a feature that is internally
powering all of Autopilot.

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:03.550
But what if you can't
yet run Autopilot?

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:06.160
Bin packing, that
annoying issue that

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:10.840
requires you to either make
sure your workload fits well

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:14.620
inside of the machine side
or manually create and manage

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:18.610
multiple node pools and play
with affinity, that can quickly

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:22.460
get out of control and become
an operational bottleneck.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:24.530
But there is a simple option--

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:28.200
configure node
auto-provisioning.

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:32.340
Node auto-provisioning is the
next generation of our cluster

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:36.570
autoscaler that not only knows
to scale your cluster using

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:41.880
the chosen VM types, but also
knows to create entire node

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:44.850
pools from scratch
that are best suited

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:47.550
for your specific workloads.

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:52.080
Node auto-provisioning makes
GKE clusters fully automatic,

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:55.770
provisioning relevant machine
types like GPUs and TPUs,

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:59.400
and removing node
pools when not needed.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:03.460
Node auto-provisioning is also
integrated with vertical pod

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:06.720
autoscaling and provisions
optimal node pools

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:09.570
ahead of vertical pod
autoscaling updates

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:12.460
to ensure quick action.

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:15.310
In practice, that
means that you're

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:18.250
more likely to have VMs
that are the right size

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:20.680
for your workloads
than what you get

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:23.980
with the manual provisioning.

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:25.420
All right, Roman.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:26.980
Those are my top three.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:29.040
What about yours?

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:30.290
ROMAN ARCEA: Thank you, Jerzy.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:33.030
I definitely have
my own list as well.

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:35.750
Let me go through some
of our latest additions

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:38.030
that we believe are
important for everyone

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:39.830
to know and understand.

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:42.650
We know that with Autopilot
and node auto-provisioning,

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:46.130
bin packing is a nonexistent
or considerably smaller

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:47.090
of a problem.

00:10:47.090 --> 00:10:49.460
But what about
application rightsizing?

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:51.830
While app rightsizing is
a challenging dimension

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:54.290
to tackle, we've been
making significant progress

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:56.670
to make it simpler for you.

00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:59.660
Let's look at the latest preview
for the multidimensional pod

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:01.130
autoscaling.

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:03.110
Horizontal pod
autoscaler is a term

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:06.710
that is familiar to
most everyone using GKE.

00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:09.830
Horizontal pod autoscaling
scales your pods horizontally,

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:12.800
adding replicas when certain
thresholds or conditions are

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:14.120
met.

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:16.730
HPA works hand-in-hand
with cluster autoscaler

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:18.860
or node auto-provisioner,
making sure

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:21.350
that resources
requested by containers

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:23.840
can be accommodated
on an existing node.

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:25.880
If this is not the
case, new nodes

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:28.030
are being added
to the node pool.

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:31.750
However, that means that the
user deploying containers

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:34.880
must have expert knowledge of
the exact amount of resources

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:37.410
to be configured
for their workloads.

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:40.230
Based on what we know of
how Kubernetes is used,

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:44.340
the right resource choice is a
true challenge that few master.

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:46.560
This is why we've
introduced the vertical pod

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.980
autoscaling, a capability that
understands the true resource

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:52.710
usage of your containers
and recommends

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:55.320
or directly adjust the
requests to get them

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:58.410
as close as possible to what
your containers actually

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:00.930
need to operate properly.

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:05.570
However, HPA and VPA did not
always play well together.

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:08.330
As HPA had targets
such as CPU, VPA

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:11.330
had trouble understanding either
resources under consumption

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:13.580
was a choice or an effect.

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:16.160
This is why we've introduced
the multidimensional pod

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:17.750
autoscaler, now in preview.

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:19.880
With a single
object, GKE can now

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.910
manage both horizontal
and vertical autoscaling.

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:26.420
While HPA continues to add
or remove pod replicas based

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:28.880
on CPU, vertical pod
autoscaling works

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:31.730
on memory, one of the most
over-provisioned resources,

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:34.670
to adjust it to requests
that meet best your container

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:38.810
actual usage needs, with this
further simplifying the app

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:40.400
rightsizing and
the off-peak hour

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:42.800
over-provisioning challenges.

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:46.130
Now, what if we told you that
you could improve your GKE

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:49.940
cluster utilization meaningfully
with a single change?

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:52.640
Please meet the
optimize-utilization profile,

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:54.260
now in GA.

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:56.510
If your applications
are ready to tolerate

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:58.790
a more aggressive cluster
autoscaler behavior,

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:01.010
this is the feature to try.

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:03.530
The optimize-utilization
profile is the second

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:06.740
in line of our autoscaling
profiles giving users

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:09.740
choice of a more active
autoscaling and scheduling

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:10.820
behavior.

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:14.570
The well-known balanced profile
optimizes for availability.

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:17.750
It scales down idle nodes
only after 10 minutes.

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:21.020
Workloads are also spread
as much as possible

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:22.730
by the scheduler.

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:24.980
Optimize-utilization
profile, on the other hand,

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:27.680
strikes a balance between
availability and cost

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:28.700
efficiency.

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:31.790
Nodes scale down after
1 minute of being idle.

00:13:31.790 --> 00:13:34.310
Aside, scheduler
tries to bin pack pods

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:37.850
from different deployments as
much as possible to add scaling

00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:40.130
down speed and
statistically maximize

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:42.590
your chances of having
empty nodes that

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:44.720
can be easily removed.

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:47.060
Where it takes the balanced
profile as much as 10

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:48.830
to 30 minutes to
scale down nodes

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:51.590
after an abrupt
stop in requests,

00:13:51.590 --> 00:13:55.730
it takes optimize-utilization
profile 1 to 3 minutes

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:57.620
to achieve the same result.

00:13:57.620 --> 00:13:59.360
The way this is
achieved is reducing

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:01.010
a number of intervals
and thresholds

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:03.740
that increase the sensitivity
of autoscaling behavior

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:06.830
to set load thresholds,
such as scale-down times,

00:14:06.830 --> 00:14:10.430
delay after add,
and scan intervals.

00:14:10.430 --> 00:14:13.010
In our tests,
optimize-utilization profile

00:14:13.010 --> 00:14:15.500
shows up as being one
of the capabilities

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:17.390
that you could adopt
for significantly

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:19.850
improved outcomes.

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:22.850
We've now had a look at
five great GKE features that

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:25.490
help improve the balance
between cost and performance out

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:26.970
of the box.

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:30.790
But how do you even know if
anything needs to be improved?

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:33.780
And what is the scale of
the opportunity at hand?

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:36.090
How do you help your
team take a step forward

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:38.910
into solving for the cultural
challenges of GKE cost

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:40.660
optimization?

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:43.900
I am thrilled to introduce
the GKE cost optimization

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:46.540
insights, now
available in preview

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:50.490
across all regions
and GKE cluster types.

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:54.820
GKE cost optimization insights
gives you a single entry point

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:57.790
into the GKE cost
optimization journey right

00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:00.260
inside the user interface.

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:03.040
Let's see how it works.

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:06.270
Let's start with a blank project
and create the first cluster.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:09.060
For this, we will use
the recently released

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:12.070
